critical eye toward printed and visual material drawn from literature, history, politics, science, and arts in the Italophone world. Conversation skills will be practiced in daily discussions and in individual or group projects and presentations. A variety of written assignments will help students increase the range, accuracy, and stylistic appropriateness of their writing. Review of select grammar topics is part of this course, as is reading a short contemporary novel.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "Satisfies Option 1.", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "Prerequisite: ITAL 2201.", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 357443, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ITAL", "catalogNbr": "2204", "titleShort": "The Cinematic Eye of Italy", "titleLong": "The Cinematic Eye of Italy", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 7589, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "11:15AM", "timeEnd": "12:05PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ie10", "firstName": "Irene", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Eibenstein-Alvisi"}], "pattern": "MWF", "facilityDescr": "Uris Hall G88", "bldgDescr": "Uris Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "URHG88", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "Course may be repeated once for credit as long as the course content is different."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This course, which is at the core of the major and minor in Italian, is designed to give students a basic grounding in some of the most important facets of Italian culture, including cinema, literature, art, and food.\u00a0 Students will trace the development of Italian national identity in literary and cinematic texts as well as across Italian photography and cuisine.\u00a0 Readings will include selections from the works of Primo Levi, Roberto Saviano, and Leonardo Sciascia.\u00a0 Students will also view films by directors such as Rossellini, de Sica, Antonioni, Bertolucci, and Sergio Leone, becoming familiar along the way with genres in Italian national cinema.\u00a0 By the end of the semester, students will have a working knowledge of the effects of geographic and national fragmentation on political life in post-Risorgimento Italy, understand the so-called Southern Question and the fraught relationship between the Italian South and wealthier Northern regions, and gain different perspectives on political life today in Italy.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, CA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "Satisfies Option 1.", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "